## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to adapt sorting techniques to handle duplicate elements efficiently and to analyze complexity trade-offs between quicksort variants and mergesort.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partition using Dutch national flag algorithm
- Average vs worst-case complexity with duplicate keys
- In-place sorting with tail recursion optimization
- Space complexity comparison between quicksort and mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi) with lt/gt pointers
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  const pivot = a[lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;

  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) swap(a, lt++, i++);
    else if (a[i] > pivot) swap(a, i, gt--);
    else i++;
  }

  // Tail recurse on smaller subarray for O(log n) space
  if (gt - lo < hi - lt) {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt + 1;
  } else {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
    hi = lt - 1;
  }

  if (lo < hi) threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi);
}
```

## Test Plan
- **All equal**: Verify no swaps and linear time
- **Already sorted**: Confirm correct partitioning and maintain sorted order
- **No duplicates**: Validate against standard quicksort behavior
- **Random with duplicates**: Compare against built-in sort for correctness
- **Large duplicate ratio**: Measure performance improvement vs standard quicksort

## Complexity Comparison
- **Three-way quicksort**: O(n log m) average, O(n²) worst, O(log n) space
- **Standard mergesort**: O(n log n) always, O(n) extra space

Optimal when m ≪ n (many duplicate keys).

## Why This Is Asked

